      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, `sflow.c:344` is precisely the case. In the old `sflow_netlink_set_attr()`, `iov_len` was set to `NLMSG_ALIGN(len)` while `iov_base` covered only `len` bytes, so `sendmsg()` read 1–3 bytes past the end of the value whenever `len` was NOT a multiple of 4.\n* `ATTR_DATA (sample-\u003eheader, header_bytes)`: over-read past the header buffer when `header_bytes % 4 !\u003d 0`\n* scalar attrs via `set_attr_int (\u0026val, sizeof(val))`: a `u16` → `NLMSG_ALIGN(2) \u003d 4` → 2 bytes read past the temporary. This is the one ASan actually aborts on.\n\nOn Coverity, I suspect it did not flag the issue because the over-read happens across the `iovec`/`sendmsg` boundary. It does not tie `iov_len` back to the size of the buffer at `iov_base` and the real buffers were quad-aligned/over-allocated, so there was no page-granular OOB. ASan\u0027s byte-granular redzones are what caught the scalar case.\n\nYes, the pad bytes are now always zero. My understanding is also that the original reason to fold the pad into the value `iovec` was to avoid leaving message bytes unset. The fix keeps that guarantee and makes it deterministic - the padding is sourced from a const zero buffer instead of whatever followed the value:\n```\nstatic const u8 nl_pad[NLMSG_ALIGNTO] \u003d { 0 };   // NLMSG_ALIGNTO \u003d 4, covers max pad of 3\n...\nu32 pad \u003d NLMSG_ALIGN (psa-\u003eval.iov_len) - psa-\u003eval.iov_len;\nif (pad)\n  {\n    iov[frag].iov_base \u003d (void *) nl_pad;   // padding sourced from zeroed buffer\n    iov[frag].iov_len  \u003d pad;\n    frag++;\n  }\n```",
